\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:sim-and-eval}

\subsection{Simulation protocol}
The estimator is evaluated on four JONSWAP directional seas and four
Pierson--Moskowitz (PM)--Stokes seas spanning the wave conditions in
Table~\ref{tab:scenarios}.  Each record is \SI{1200}{s} long at
\SI{200}{Hz}; the magnetometer is sampled independently at \SI{25}{Hz} and
held between updates.  The simulator provides reference displacement,
velocity, acceleration, and attitude together with body-frame specific force,
angular rate, and magnetic-field measurements.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Stationary simulation scenarios.}
  \label{tab:scenarios}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
    \toprule
    Scenario & $H_s$ [m] & $T_p$ [s] & Nominal direction \\
    \midrule
    W1 & 0.27 & 3.0  & $+30^\circ$ \\
    W2 & 1.50 & 5.7  & $-30^\circ$ \\
    W3 & 4.00 & 8.5  & $+30^\circ$ \\
    W4 & 8.50 & 11.4 & $-30^\circ$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The inertial measurements include white noise, residual constant bias, and
slow bias drift.  The magnetometer additionally includes hard-iron residual,
scale and cross-axis error, misalignment, and slow drift.  The principal
per-axis error levels are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:sim_constants}; the
accelerometer truth retains a random-walk bias while the filter models the
residual about the temperature-calibration curve as a slow first-order
Gauss--Markov process, so the validation includes process-model mismatch.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Principal simulation timing and sensor-error levels.}
  \label{tab:sim_constants}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
    \toprule
    Quantity & Value & Notes \\
    \midrule
    IMU rate & \SI{200}{\hertz} & prediction and accel update \\
    Magnetometer rate & \SI{25}{\hertz} & asynchronous update \\
    $S$ pseudo-update period & \SI{15}{\milli\second} & three IMU samples \\
    Accel white noise & \SI{0.0148}{\meter\per\second\squared} & RMS per sample \\
    Accel bias RW & \SI{5e-4}{\meter\per\second\squared\per\second\tothe{0.5}} & truth model \\
    Gyro white noise & \SI{0.00157}{\radian\per\second} & RMS per sample \\
    Gyro bias RW & \SI{1e-5}{\radian\per\second\per\second\tothe{0.5}} & truth model \\
    Mag white noise & \SI{0.80}{\micro\tesla} & RMS per mag sample \\
    Mag hard-iron residual & \SI{2.0}{\micro\tesla} & half-range \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Temperature is not varied in the scored simulations, and no separate thermal
calibration-error, lag, or hysteresis experiment is included.  The
finite-$\tau_b$ temperature-centered residual is therefore a structural sensor
model used by the estimator and stability analysis, not a demonstrated
temperature-compensation performance contribution.  Empirically, the relevant
result here is robustness to the deliberate mismatch between that residual
model and the random-walk accelerometer-bias truth process.

All deterministic and ensemble comparisons use the final \SI{900}{s} of each
record, excluding startup while retaining a long common scoring interval.
Reported metrics include per-axis and three-dimensional displacement RMS,
attitude RMS, and wave-direction error.  Both OU--II and OU--III obtain their
operating period from the same wave-band zero-crossing estimate; this matched
input is required for the family comparison to isolate the additional
integral-displacement state rather than a difference in tuning signal.

