\subsection{Paired ensemble and ablations}
Ten predeclared seed triplets independently vary the wave realization, sensor
noise and drift, and residual-error initialization.  Each triplet is shared
between OU--II, OU--III, and the tuning ablations.  The declared primary
endpoint is the seed-level mean normalized vertical-displacement RMS error over
the four stationary JONSWAP seas.  Scenario summaries report mean $\pm$
sample standard deviation across seeds; paired differences are accompanied by
95\% percentile-bootstrap intervals obtained by resampling seed-level
paired differences.  An exact paired sign-flip test is used as a secondary
check on the primary endpoint.  Other comparisons are descriptive.
\label{par:companion-inference}

\paragraph{Fixed references and adaptation channels}
\label{par:fixed-points}
FixedNominal freezes the operating point calibrated from a noise-free
$H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$, $T_p=\SI{5.7}{s}$ record.  FixedOracle uses the same
calibration procedure for the sea being scored, or for the known endpoint of
the transition; it is therefore a scenario-matched, nondeployable reference,
not an error-minimizing oracle.  The OU--III channel ablation separates
adaptation of $(\tau,\sigma_{aw})$ from adaptation of the integral
regularization scale $r_S$: \emph{$r_S$ only} freezes the OU parameters while
$r_S$ remains adaptive, whereas \emph{OU only} adapts the OU parameters with
$r_S$ frozen.
\label{par:channel-ablation}

The deployed regularization law is
$r_S=\clip(0.35\,\sigma_{aw}\tau^3,0.4,400)$.  A matched control repeats the
Adaptive and FixedNominal modes without periodic re-alignment of the latent
acceleration covariance, allowing the effect of that covariance-maintenance
policy to be separated from the effect of online tuning.

\paragraph{Non-stationary case}
The controlled transition is a $C^2$ quintic crossfade from
$(H_s,T_p)=(\SI{1.5}{m},\SI{5.7}{s})$ to
$(\SI{4.0}{m},\SI{11.4}{s})$ over $t=420$--$780$~s, using independently
phase-randomized endpoint records and derivative-consistent velocity and
acceleration.  It is a kinematically closed crossfade rather than a continuously
evolving wave spectrum; both endpoint spectra coexist during the blend and the
intermediate sea need not have a unique $T_p$.  Consequently the whole-window
normalization by the final $H_s$ is retained only for continuity.  Interpretation
uses separate pure-start, crossfade, and pure-endpoint intervals and the
scale-free ratio of estimation RMS to reference-displacement RMS over the same
interval.

